{70 = 70-79}
70.	@q{“But I am the beautiful and clever Bird! Fine artistry went into my adornment. 
71.	But no skill has been expended on your holy shaping! 
72.	Strutting about in the royal palace is my glory; my warbling is considered a decoration in the courtyard. 
73.	The sound I produce, in all its sweetness, is a delight for the person of @wRN{Šulgi}, 
74.	son of @wDN{Enlil}. 
75.	Fruits and produce of gardens and orchards are the enormous daily offerings due to me. 
76.	Groats, flour, malt, 
77.	hulled barley and emmer (?) are sweet things to my mouth. 
78.	How do you not recognise my superiority from this? 
79.	Bow your neck to the ground!”} 

{80 = 80-85}
80.	Thus Bird insulted Fish on that occasion. Fish became angry, 
81.	and, trusting in its heroic strength and solidness, swept across the bottom like a heavy rain cloud. 
82.	It took up the quarrel. 
83.	It did not take to heart the insults that Bird had cast at it. 
84.	It could not bring itself to submit, but spoke unrestrainedly. 
85.	Again Fish replied to Bird: 

{86 = 86-94}
86.	@q[Fish,Bird]{“Chopped-off beak and legs, deformed feet, cleft mouth, thin tongue! You clatter away in your ignorance, 
87.	with never any reflection! Gluttonous, malformed, filling the courtyard with droppings! 
88.	The little sweeper-boy sets nets in the house and chases you with ropes. 
89.	The baker, the brewer, the porter, all those who live in the house are annoyed with you. 
90.	Bird, you have not examined the question of my greatness; you have not taken due account of my nature. 
91.	You could not understand my weakness and my strength; yet you spoke inflammatory words. 
92.	Once you have really looked into my achievements, you will be greatly humbled. 
93.	Your speech contains grave errors; 
94.	you have not given it due consideration.”} 

{95 = 95-101}
