99.	they rejoice over me and take care of me. Bird, whatever great deeds you may have achieved, 
100.	I will teach you their pretentiousness. 
101.	I shall hand back to you in your turn your haughtiness and mendacious speech.”} 

{102 = 102-115}
102.	Thereupon Fish conceived a plot against Bird. Silently, furtively, it slithered alongside. 
103.	When Bird rose up from her nest to fetch food for her young, 
104.	Fish searched for the most discreet of silent places. 
105.	It turned her well-built nest of brushwood into a haunted house. 
106.	It destroyed her well-built house, and tore down her storeroom. 
107.	It smashed the eggs she had laid and threw them into the sea. 
108.	Thus Fish struck at Bird, and then fled into the waters. 
109.	Then Bird came, lion-faced and with an eagle's talons, flapping its wings towards its nest. 
110.	It stopped in mid-flight. Like a hurricane whirling in the midst of heaven, 
111.	it circled in the sky. Bird, 
112.	looking about for its nest, 
113.	spread wide its limbs. 
114.	It trampled over the broad plain after its well-built nest of brushwood. 
115.	Its voice shrieked into the interior of heaven like the Mistress's. 

